Teaching a humanoid to move a box it can’t lift? This new work from the University of Tokyo shows it’s possible—by learning to *pivot-walk* heavy loads, just like humans rocking furniture.

Instead of separate phases, a single RL policy fuses footwork, balance and hand forces so the robot tips, swings, and advances a 14.9 kg box in one continuous motion. In sim, it pivoted 4.12 m in 20 s with 41 swings and zero failures. Even after removing all object sensing, the gait survived—on real hardware, five runs each moved the box >1 m with fully blind proprioceptive control.

No external sensors, no explicit contact planning—just learned, whole-body coordination. It’s a leap for practical, human-like manipulation in service robots.

